{d}x & =\\int \\frac{5}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}|3x+4|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}K$ for another arbitrary positive constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}|3x+4|+C & =\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}|3x+4|+\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}K \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}(\\operatorname{ln}|3x+4|+\\operatorname{ln}K) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}(K|3x+4|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{5}{3x+4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{5}{3}\\operatorname{ln}(K|3x+4|).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bdbfa506-08a1-5e43-b958-2ae373df775d" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Linear Rational Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e65f7bd-7d79-529c-8606-3bf7dbebe41e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|2x+3|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244442} {:answer/id #uuid "365d1035-7911-599d-868a-0bc00a22e451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|2x+3|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244794} {:answer/id #uuid "485459c8-f996-577c-b029-238000af5c8a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{|2x+3|}{K})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244958} {:answer/id #uuid "6fe6a02d-46ef-5a0d-bd2e-0802888f9713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|2x+3|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245313} {:answer/id #uuid "cd2a865c-54aa-5f47-9c00-c3e7a1dee25a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{K}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|2x+3|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246142}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fe6a02d-46ef-5a0d-bd2e-0802888f9713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|2x+3|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245313} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2b53b2b-7028-5429-8d6f-3df36b486110" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3442786-8423-543c-a685-0f42805b9492" :question/math-academy-id "q-113532" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Assume that $K$ is an arbitrary positive constant." :question/worked-solution "First, let $u=2x+3.$ Then differentiating with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWe can now rewrite the integral in terms of $u,$ solve it, and write the final answer in terms of $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can further simplify the above by writing $C=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K$ for another arbitrary constant $K,$ and then we can combine the two terms using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|+C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡K \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+3|+\\operatorname{ln}⁡K) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|2x+3|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we finally conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{2x+3}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(K|2x+3|).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uu